    "evidence_text": "detection methods perform in a cross-lingual setting, as the multilingual datasets available today are relatively small, and specific to a single target , .\n\nis split into a multilingual training set and into multiple test sets to evaluate zero-shot cross-lingual and cross-target transfer.\n\nIn addition, multilingual Bert can generalize to a degree both cross-lingually and in a cross-target setting.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: Multilingual BERT Baseline ::: Results\tTable TABREF36 shows the results for the cross-lingual setting.\n\nSimilar to the cross-lingual setting, M-Bert performs worse in a cross-target setting but easily surpasses the majority class baselines.\n\nWhile the effect is negligible in most settings, the cross-lingual performance clearly increases when all questions are\n\nThe effect of missing questions is especially strong in the supervised and cross-lingual settings.\n\nAn M-Bert model that learns target embeddings instead of encoding a question performs clearly worse in the supervised and cross-lingual settings.\n\nOn the other hand, in the cross-lingual and cross-target settings, the mean score drops by 6\u20138 percentage points compared to the supervised setting; while zero-shot transfer is possible to a degree, it can still be improved.",
